Here is a CSS code that will make all text boxes visible on the page:

```css
input[type="text"],
textarea {
    visibility: visible;
}
```
This code targets all `input` elements with the `type` attribute set to `"text"` and all `textarea` elements on the page. It then sets the `visibility` property to `visible`, ensuring that these elements are visible to the user.

Here's a very basic Express (a popular Node.js web framework) app:
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello World!');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Example app listening on port 3000!');
});

This will start a server and listen on port 3000. It responds with "Hello World!" for requests to the homepage (/).
You would run it with node app.js (where app.js is the name of the file containing this code).
Obviously this is a very simple example, you'd add more routes, serve static files, connect to a database, etc. for a real app. But this gives you the basic structure.
